#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""piboy-batd - a fuel gauge for the XPi pack, to replace the MCU's percent.

The MCU's own `percent` is a voltage lookup. On a handheld that is the one thing
it cannot be: terminal voltage under load is dominated by IR drop, so the number
climbs when you drop to the menu and falls when a game starts, and nothing about
that motion is charge. This integrates current instead, and uses voltage only as
a slow anchor - the standard complementary filter, and the same approach the
Batocera port took, arrived at from the same complaint.

What the hardware gives us (driver/xpi_gamecon.c, via power_supply):

    voltage_now   batt_val*5 + 2950 mV   -> 1S Li-ion, 2950..4225 mV, 5 mV steps
    current_now   (signed char)*50 mA    -> +-6350 mA, 50 mA steps, - = discharge
    status        derived from VBus, not from the sign of the current

The 50 mA quantisation is the whole design problem. At a typical 600 mA draw one
LSB is 8% of the reading, and if the MCU truncates rather than rounds, the error
is a bias rather than noise: up to 50 mA held for six hours is 300 mAh, 7.5% of
the pack, all in one direction. Coulomb counting alone therefore drifts, however
carefully it is done, and must be re-anchored against voltage.

Anchoring cannot wait for the pack to rest, because on a Pi 4 it never does -
idle is still ~450 mA. So OCV is inferred instead:

    OCV = V_terminal - I * R_pack

At 600 mA and 100 mohm that is a 60 mV correction, twelve times the voltage LSB,
so it is well resolved. The anchor is then weighted by how much the OCV curve is
actually saying: steep near full and near empty, nearly flat from 30-70%, where
a millivolt of error is several percent of charge and the anchor is worth little.

Two hard anchors do the real work, and neither needs the curve:
  * charge termination - status Full means the pack IS full, so set 100%
  * the persisted state file, for charge carried across a reboot

CALIBRATION. OCV_TABLE and R_PACK_MOHM below are generic 1S Li-ion values, good
enough to run today and not good enough to trust. CAPACITY_MAH is the Batocera
port's measurement of a stock pack (~4000 mAh usable against a 4900 mAh label),
not a measurement of ours. To calibrate against this actual unit:

    piboy-batd --log /var/log/piboy-batt.csv          # one full charge -> shutdown
    sudo scripts/fit-battery-curve.py /var/log/piboy-batt.csv
    piboy-batd --replay /var/log/piboy-batt.csv       # check the fit, then restart

The fitter writes /var/lib/piboy/battery-cal.json and this daemon prefers it
over the constants below, so calibrating never means editing code - which also
means --replay is checking the same numbers the daemon will actually run.

Output goes to the driver, not to a file of our own: writing a percent to
/sys/kernel/xpi_gamecon/capacity_override makes POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CAPACITY
report it, so EmulationStation, RetroArch, upower, logind and piboy-fand all
see the good number through the interface they already read. Writing -1 hands
control back to the MCU, and the driver expires the override after 60s, so
killing this daemon restores the old behaviour rather than freezing a stale
percentage on screen.

It also owns the clean shutdown on an empty pack - see the constants below for
why that lives here rather than in piboy-fand, which keeps only the warnings.

Resuming from the state file is checked, not trusted: the pack may have been
charged while the handheld was off, and no amount of integrating can discover
that. The first sample after a resume compares the saved charge against OCV and
throws it away if they disagree by more than RESUME_TOL_PCT.

Known gap: a resume that survives that check is still a charge recorded before
an unknown amount of time powered down, during which the board keeps drawing.
The OCV anchor pulls that back, but slowly - a few minutes on the steep parts of
the curve, longer on the plateau. If a --replay of a real resume shows that is
too slow, the fix is a faster anchor for the first minute or two. Measure first.
"""
import argparse, csv, json, os, signal, subprocess, sys, time

PSY         = '/sys/class/power_supply/xpi-battery'
OUT         = '/sys/kernel/xpi_gamecon/capacity_override'
RAW_PCT     = '/sys/kernel/xpi_gamecon/percent'
STATE       = '/var/lib/piboy/fuel-gauge.json'
CAL         = '/var/lib/piboy/battery-cal.json'

CAPACITY_MAH = 4000.0   # usable, NOT the 4900 mAh on the label. See above.
R_PACK_MOHM  = 100.0    # cell + wiring + connector + protection FET
SAMPLE_S     = 1.0
TAU_S        = 300.0    # OCV anchor time constant, at full confidence
SLOPE_REF    = 10.0     # mV per % at which the OCV anchor is fully trusted
STEP_MA      = 200.0    # skip the anchor when the load just moved this much
SAVE_MAH     = CAPACITY_MAH / 100.0   # persist once the estimate moves this far
SAVE_MAX_S   = 300.0                  # ...and at least this often while draining
PUBLISH_S    = 20.0                   # refresh the override well inside its 60s expiry

# Clean shutdown on an empty pack. This lives here, not in piboy-fand, because
# fand exits when pwm1 is unwritable and then burns its systemd start limit -
# which would make protection against an unclean power cut contingent on the
# FAN hardware. This daemon owns the gauge, samples every second, and fails for
# its own reasons only.
#
# Two triggers, and they are less redundant than they look. The percentage is
# this gauge's coulomb-counted estimate; the voltage floor is the raw rail and
# depends on no calibration at all, so it still fires if the gauge is wrong -
# which, until a real discharge has been captured, it may well be.
BATT_SHUTDOWN_PCT = 3       # percent, at or below, while discharging
SHUTDOWN_SAMPLES  = 10      # consecutive samples (~10s at SAMPLE_S)
VOLT_FLOOR_MV     = 3300.0  # ~3.36V open-circuit under a 600mA load through
                            # ~100 mohm, i.e. genuinely empty
VOLT_SAMPLES      = 30      # ~30s: a load spike sags the rail briefly and must
                            # not be allowed to power the device off mid-game

# Force the status bar back on when the pack gets this low. Launching a game
# hides the bar (runcommand-onstart.sh) because `layer: overlay` costs ~7.7% of
# a core in composition - but the bar is also where the low-battery warning
# lives, and hiding it would blind the player during the activity that drains
# fastest. So the saving is given back for the last few percent only.
# Matches waybar's own `critical` state in waybar.json, deliberately: the bar
# reappears already pulsing red rather than showing a calm number.
BAR_SHOW_PCT = 10
STATUSBAR    = '/opt/retropie/configs/all/wayland/statusbar.sh'

# How far the resumed charge may sit from what OCV says before it is discarded.
# Wide, because on the 30-70% plateau the generic curve is genuinely poor and a
# false reseed would throw away a good integration. A pack charged while the
# handheld was off misses by far more than this.
RESUME_TOL_PCT = 20

# Resting OCV (mV) at each 1S SoC. Generic LiCoO2/LiPo - replace with a fit.
OCV_TABLE = [
    (0,   3300), (5,   3550), (10,  3630), (15,  3670), (20,  3700),
    (30,  3750), (40,  3790), (50,  3820), (60,  3870), (70,  3950),
    (80,  4020), (90,  4110), (95,  4150), (100, 4200),
]


def log(msg):
    print(f'piboy-batd: {msg}', flush=True)


def load_cal(path):
    """Calibration measured on this actual pack. Absent is normal and falls back
    to the generic constants above; present but malformed is a mistake worth
    stopping for, rather than silently reverting to numbers that are not ours."""
    global CAPACITY_MAH, R_PACK_MOHM, OCV_TABLE, SAVE_MAH
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            c = json.load(f)
    except OSError:
        log(f'no {path} - using built-in generic constants, NOT calibrated')
        return
    except ValueError as e:              # JSONDecodeError: truncated, half-written
        sys.exit(f'{path} is not valid JSON: {e}')
    try:
        CAPACITY_MAH = float(c['capacity_mah'])
        R_PACK_MOHM = float(c['r_pack_mohm'])
        OCV_TABLE = [(float(soc), float(mv)) for soc, mv in c['ocv_table']]
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError) as e:
        sys.exit(f'{path} is malformed: {e}')
    SAVE_MAH = CAPACITY_MAH / 100.0
    log(f'calibrated from {path}: {CAPACITY_MAH:.0f} mAh, {R_PACK_MOHM:.0f} mohm, '
        f'{len(OCV_TABLE)} curve points')


def read_int(name):
    """One sysfs integer, or None. A read can fail mid-rmmod; that is not fatal.
    None is distinct from 0 here - 0 is a legitimate current."""
    try:
        with open(f'{PSY}/{name}') as f:
            return int(f.read().strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def read_raw_pct():
    """The MCU's own percent, straight from the driver.

    NOT power_supply's `capacity`: once this daemon starts publishing, the
    driver answers that property with our own override, so logging it would
    make the calibration capture a recording of ourselves - and --replay would
    then compare the gauge against the gauge and call it agreement."""
    try:
        with open(RAW_PCT) as f:
            return int(f.read().strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def read_str(name):
    try:
        with open(f'{PSY}/{name}') as f:
            return f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ''


def check_table():
    """A table that is not increasing in both columns cannot be inverted: the
    bracket search would match the wrong segment and hand back a negative slope,
    which becomes a negative anchor weight and drives the estimate the wrong way.
    A fitted table is one paste away from being wrong, so refuse it loudly."""
    # Fewer than two points is not a curve, and the pairwise loop below would
    # wave it through: every reading would then hit a clamp and the gauge would
    # have no anchor at all. A one-slice fit can produce exactly this.
    if len(OCV_TABLE) < 2:
        sys.exit(f'OCV_TABLE has {len(OCV_TABLE)} points - need at least 2')
    for (s0, v0), (s1, v1) in zip(OCV_TABLE, OCV_TABLE[1:]):
        if s1 <= s0 or v1 < v0:
            sys.exit(f'OCV_TABLE is not monotonic at ({s0}, {v0}) -> ({s1}, {v1})')


def soc_from_ocv(mv):
    """Invert OCV_TABLE. Returns (soc_percent, local slope in mV per %).

    check_table() guarantees the voltage column is non-decreasing and the two
    clamps below put mv strictly inside it, so some consecutive pair always
    brackets it and the loop always returns."""
    # Clamp to the table's OWN ends, not to 0/100. The built-in table happens to
    # span the full range, but a fitted one only covers the slices that had
    # enough load variation to fit - often 8%..92%. Returning 0.0 there would
    # anchor the gauge to empty with a tenth of the pack left, and trip every
    # low-battery policy on the device.
    #
    # Slope 0, so the anchor is OFF outside the fitted range. Past the ends the
    # table has no opinion about where the charge is - only that it is at least
    # this far out - and pulling the estimate to the endpoint at full confidence
    # would be the same mistake in a smaller size: a genuinely full pack dragged
    # down to 92% moments after charge termination set it to 100%. The endpoint
    # SoC is still returned, because seed_from_ocv has to start somewhere, and
    # the 'Full' hard anchor corrects the top end for free.
    if mv <= OCV_TABLE[0][1]:
        return OCV_TABLE[0][0], 0.0
    if mv >= OCV_TABLE[-1][1]:
        return OCV_TABLE[-1][0], 0.0
    for (s0, v0), (s1, v1) in zip(OCV_TABLE, OCV_TABLE[1:]):
        if v0 <= mv <= v1:
            if v1 == v0:
                # Dead flat: this voltage spans a range of charge, so it carries
                # no information. Slope 0 turns the anchor off here, which is the
                # honest answer rather than a guess at the midpoint.
                return (s0 + s1) / 2.0, 0.0
            frac = (mv - v0) / (v1 - v0)
            return s0 + frac * (s1 - s0), (v1 - v0) / (s1 - s0)


def mah(dt_s, i0, i1):
    """Charge moved between two current samples, trapezoid. The load genuinely
    moves between samples, so neither endpoint alone is right."""
    return (i0 + i1) / 2.0 * (dt_s / 3600.0)


class Gauge:
    """Charge in mAh, integrated and slowly corrected toward the OCV estimate."""

    def __init__(self, charge_mah=None):
        self.charge = charge_mah          # None until the first sample seeds it
        self.reported = None
        self.prev_i = None
        self.check_resume = charge_mah is not None

    def seed_from_ocv(self, v_mv, i_ma):
        soc, _ = soc_from_ocv(v_mv - i_ma * R_PACK_MOHM / 1000.0)
        self.charge = soc / 100.0 * CAPACITY_MAH
        log(f'seeded from OCV: {soc:.1f}% ({self.charge:.0f} mAh)')

    def update(self, dt, v_mv, i_ma, status):
        if self.charge is None:
            self.seed_from_ocv(v_mv, i_ma)
            self.prev_i = i_ma
            return self.percent()

        # A resumed charge describes the pack as it was when this daemon last
        # ran - and the pack may well have been CHARGED since, which is the
        # ordinary thing to do with a handheld that just shut down empty.
        # Nothing in the integration can discover that, and percent()'s
        # never-climb-while-draining rule would then pin the published percent
        # to the stale low value for the entire session: the device would boot
        # on a full pack, report 3%, and power itself off ten seconds later.
        # So check the resumed figure against OCV once, and drop it if the pack
        # is plainly not where we left it.
        if self.check_resume:
            self.check_resume = False
            soc, _ = soc_from_ocv(v_mv - i_ma * R_PACK_MOHM / 1000.0)
            stale = self.charge / CAPACITY_MAH * 100.0
            if abs(soc - stale) > RESUME_TOL_PCT:
                log(f'resumed at {stale:.0f}% but OCV says {soc:.0f}% - the pack '
                    f'moved while we were off, discarding the saved charge')
                self.seed_from_ocv(v_mv, i_ma)
                self.prev_i = i_ma
                return self.percent()

        self.charge += mah(dt, self.prev_i if self.prev_i is not None else i_ma,
                           i_ma)

        # Hard anchor. Charge termination is a statement about the pack, not an
        # inference from it, so it overrides everything and costs no curve.
        if status == 'Full':
            self.charge = CAPACITY_MAH
        else:
            self._anchor(dt, v_mv, i_ma)

        self.charge = min(max(self.charge, 0.0), CAPACITY_MAH)
        self.prev_i = i_ma
        return self.percent()

    def _anchor(self, dt, v_mv, i_ma):
        # A load step means the terminal voltage has not settled and the IR model
        # is being asked about a transient. Wait for the next sample.
        if self.prev_i is not None and abs(i_ma - self.prev_i) > STEP_MA:
            return

        soc, slope = soc_from_ocv(v_mv - i_ma * R_PACK_MOHM / 1000.0)

        # Trust the anchor in proportion to what the curve can resolve. On the
        # 30-70% plateau the slope collapses and so does the weight, which is
        # correct: there, voltage genuinely does not know the charge.
        alpha = min(1.0, min(1.0, slope / SLOPE_REF) * dt / TAU_S)
        self.charge += (soc / 100.0 * CAPACITY_MAH - self.charge) * alpha

    def percent(self):
        pct = min(max(int(round(self.charge / CAPACITY_MAH * 100.0)), 0), 100)
        # Never let the displayed number climb while draining. The anchor can
        # legitimately push the estimate up; showing that is the exact jitter
        # this daemon exists to remove.
        if self.reported is not None and self.prev_i is not None \
                and self.prev_i < 0 and pct > self.reported:
            pct = self.reported
        self.reported = pct
        return pct


def load_state():
    try:
        with open(STATE) as f:
            return float(json.load(f)['charge_mah'])
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError):
        return None


def save_state(charge):
    """Rename-over-fsync: this one is on the SD card and must survive a crash."""
    tmp = f'{STATE}.tmp'
    try:
        with open(tmp, 'w') as f:
            f.write(json.dumps({'charge_mah': round(charge, 1)}))
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        os.replace(tmp, STATE)
    except OSError as e:
        log(f'could not save state: {e}')


def run(logfile):
    if not os.path.isdir(PSY):
        sys.exit(f'{PSY} not present - is xpi_gamecon loaded?')
    if not os.access(OUT, os.W_OK):
        sys.exit(f'{OUT} not writable - old xpi_gamecon without capacity_override?')
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(STATE), exist_ok=True)

    gauge = Gauge(load_state())
    if gauge.charge is not None:
        log(f'resumed at {gauge.charge:.0f} mAh')

    fh = None
    if logfile:
        fh = open(logfile, 'a', newline='')
        writer = csv.writer(fh)
        if fh.tell() == 0:
            writer.writerow(['wall', 'mono', 'mv', 'ma', 'mcu_pct', 'status'])

    stop = []
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda *_: stop.append(True))
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, lambda *_: stop.append(True))

    last = time.monotonic()
    written = saved_charge = None
    last_save = last_pub = 0.0
    low_pct = low_mv = 0
    bar_forced = False
    while not stop:
        time.sleep(SAMPLE_S)
        now = time.monotonic()
        dt, last = now - last, now

        uv, ua = read_int('voltage_now'), read_int('current_now')
        if uv is None or ua is None:
            continue
        mv, ma = uv / 1000.0, ua / 1000.0
        status = read_str('status')
        if status == 'Unknown':          # no valid packet yet
            continue

        pct = gauge.update(dt, mv, ma, status)

        # On change, and periodically regardless. The driver drops the override
        # 60s after the last write so a dead daemon cannot freeze the gauge -
        # which means a live one has to keep saying so, and on the OCV plateau
        # the percent can hold still for several minutes. Refreshes are cheap:
        # the driver only emits a uevent when the value actually changes.
        if pct != written or now - last_pub > PUBLISH_S:
            try:
                with open(OUT, 'w') as f:
                    f.write(f'{pct}\n')
                written, last_pub = pct, now
            except OSError as e:
                log(f'could not write {OUT}: {e}')

        if fh:
            writer.writerow([f'{time.time():.1f}', f'{now:.1f}', f'{mv:.0f}',
                             f'{ma:.0f}', read_raw_pct(), status])
            fh.flush()

        # The state file is on the SD card, so persist on real movement rather
        # than on a timer: 1% of the pack, with a ceiling so a slow drain still
        # checkpoints. A clean stop saves below; only a yanked battery loses it.
        if saved_charge is None or abs(gauge.charge - saved_charge) >= SAVE_MAH \
                or (now - last_save > SAVE_MAX_S and gauge.charge != saved_charge):
            save_state(gauge.charge)
            saved_charge, last_save = gauge.charge, now

        # The shutdown watches the gauge's own estimate, not the number we
        # publish: percent() deliberately refuses to let the displayed value
        # climb while draining, and a smoothing rule for a display must never be
        # the thing that decides to cut power.
        est_pct = gauge.charge / CAPACITY_MAH * 100.0

        # Edge-triggered, not level-triggered: forced ONCE per excursion below
        # the threshold, so the player can still hide the bar again by hand
        # afterwards and it will stay hidden. Re-arms when the pack is no
        # longer discharging, i.e. when it is put back on the charger.
        if status == 'Discharging' and est_pct <= BAR_SHOW_PCT:
            if not bar_forced:
                bar_forced = True
                log(f'battery {est_pct:.0f}% - forcing the status bar back on')
                subprocess.run([STATUSBAR, 'show'], check=False,
                               stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                               stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        else:
            bar_forced = False

        if status == 'Discharging':
            low_pct = low_pct + 1 if est_pct <= BATT_SHUTDOWN_PCT else 0
            low_mv = low_mv + 1 if mv <= VOLT_FLOOR_MV else 0
            if low_pct >= SHUTDOWN_SAMPLES or low_mv >= VOLT_SAMPLES:
                log(f'battery empty ({est_pct:.1f}%, {mv:.0f} mV) - powering off')
                save_state(gauge.charge)
                # Hand capacity back before the frontend sees a dying system, so
                # a reboot does not start up believing the override.
                try:
                    with open(OUT, 'w') as f:
                        f.write('-1\n')
                except OSError:
                    pass
                subprocess.run(['systemctl', 'poweroff'], check=False)
                return
        else:
            low_pct = low_mv = 0

    save_state(gauge.charge)
    # Hand capacity back to the MCU rather than leaving our last value to expire.
    try:
        with open(OUT, 'w') as f:
            f.write('-1\n')
    except OSError:
        pass
    if fh:
        fh.close()
    log('stopped')


def replay(path):
    """Re-run the filter over a logged discharge. The only way to check a change
    to the model without spending a battery on it."""
    gauge, rows = Gauge(), []
    with open(path) as f:
        for r in csv.DictReader(f):
            rows.append((float(r['mono']), float(r['mv']), float(r['ma']),
                         int(r['mcu_pct'] or 0), r['status']))
    if not rows:
        sys.exit('no rows')

    print(f'{"hours":>7} {"mV":>6} {"mA":>7} {"mcu%":>5} {"gauge%":>7}')
    prev_t, drawn = rows[0][0], 0.0
    step = max(1, len(rows) // 40)
    for n, (t, mv, ma, mcu, status) in enumerate(rows):
        dt = t - prev_t
        drawn -= mah(dt, rows[max(n - 1, 0)][2], ma)
        pct = gauge.update(dt, mv, ma, status)
        prev_t = t
        if n % step == 0:
            print(f'{(t - rows[0][0]) / 3600:7.2f} {mv:6.0f} {ma:7.0f} '
                  f'{mcu:5d} {pct:7d}')

    print(f'\n{(rows[-1][0] - rows[0][0]) / 3600:.2f} h, {drawn:.0f} mAh drawn, '
          f'gauge ended at {gauge.percent()}%, MCU at {rows[-1][3]}%')


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    g = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    g.add_argument('--log', metavar='CSV', help='also append raw samples, for calibration')
    g.add_argument('--replay', metavar='CSV', help='re-run the filter over a log and exit')
    ap.add_argument('--cal', metavar='JSON', default=CAL,
                    help=f'calibration from fit-battery-curve.py (default {CAL})')
    a = ap.parse_args()
    load_cal(a.cal)
    check_table()
    replay(a.replay) if a.replay else run(a.log)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
